 F means either S < X < F or F < X < S. F=1, so only F < X < S => 1 < X < S. So Xena right of seat1, Soren right of Xena.\\\\n5 Iris sat between Pia and Xena. Means Iris somewhere between Pia and Xena. Need either Pia < Iris < Xena or Xena < Iris < Pia. Also Iris next to Xena. Need combine with Pia < Rhea < Xena from 2. Since Pia < Rhea < Xena. Iris between Pia and Xena. If Iris < Xena and > Pia, possible; if Iris > Xena then would require Xena < Iris < Pia impossible because Pia < Xena. So Pia < Iris < Xena. And Iris next to Xena => Iris = Xena-1. Also Pia < Iris. Rhea between Pia and Xena too: Pia < Rhea < Xena. Since Iris = Xena-1, Rhea must be < Xena and could be < Iris or between Pia and Iris? \\\\\\\"Rhea between Xena and Pia\\\\\\\" only Pia < Rhea < Xena. It doesn't say Rhea not equal Iris. But each person unique, so Rhea distinct from Iris. Thus Pia < Rhea < Xena, Pia < Iris = Xena-1. Rhea can be seat before Iris or between Pia and Iris? Need also Xena between Soren and Faye: Faye seat1 < Xena < Soren. Soren to right of Xena. Faye left of Iris satisfied.\\\\n\\\\nNeed assign 6 seats. Let's derive.\\\\n\\\\nF=1. Need Xena >1 and Soren > Xena. Iris = X-1 (adjacent left of Xena because Iris between Pia and Xena and Pia left). Also Pia < Iris. Rhea between Pia and Xena. Since Iris = X-1, Rhea must be < Xena and could be < Iris or between Pia and Iris? Need also Pia < Rhea. Seats.\\\\n\\\\nRemaining seats 2-6 for I,X,R,P,S with X=I+1, S>X, P<I, P<R<X.\\\\n\\\\nSince F=1. Need Xena >1 and Soren > Xena. Iris = X-1. Also Pia < Iris. Rhea between Pia and Xena. Since Iris = X-1, Rhea must be < Xena and could be < Iris or between Pia and Iris? Need also Pia < Rhea. Seats.\\\\n\\\\nX cannot be 2? If X=2, I=1 but F=1 conflict. X=3 => I=2. Need P<2 and R<3, P<R. Available seats for P,R,S: seats4,5,6 plus? P/R must be <3, only seat? F=1, I=2, X=3, no seat left left of I except none. P<2 impossible (seat1 occupied F). So X not3.\\\\nX=4 => I=3. Need P<3, R<4, P<R. Seats av